<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Weather Report – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Weather Report</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Will the Weather Change Tomorrow?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Discuss plans for a trip.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can present weather survey results with visual support (e.g., 'Most students like sunny days for outdoor activities.').
Can ask and clarify directions for outdoor activities (e.g., 'How is the weather today for going outside?').
Can discuss trip plans with classmates, based on the weather report (e.g., 'Let's visit the park when it is sunny.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing four large flashcards depicting different weather conditions (e.g., "sunny," "rainy," "windy," "cloudy"). The teacher asks students, "What's the weather like today?" and encourages choral and individual responses. Then, the teacher shows a picture of a calendar set to "Tomorrow" and points to a cloud with a lightning bolt, asking, "What will the weather be like tomorrow?" to activate prior knowledge of future predictions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences using the future simple tense for weather predictions. For example, pointing to a 'sunny' flashcard, the teacher says clearly, "It will be sunny tomorrow." The teacher then points to a 'rainy' flashcard and says, "It will be rainy tomorrow. You should bring an umbrella!" The teacher emphasizes intonation in weather predictions, especially the last word of the sentence (e.g., 'It will be <b>sunny</b>.'). The teacher writes "ther-mo-stat" on the board and claps for each syllable to segment the word, reinforcing phonemic awareness.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of future predictions and recommendations.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'It will be cloudy,' does it mean it's cloudy now or later?" (Expected answer: Later / Tomorrow)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If it looks like rain, what should you bring?" (Expected answer: An umbrella)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Weather Report".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short dialogue for an interview or conversation projected on the board, featuring two friends discussing a weekend trip based on a simplified weather forecast. The dialogue includes future simple tense, prepositions of place, and modal verbs for recommendations.
                    <br><i>Example Dialogue Snippet:</i>
                    <br><i>Maria: "What's the weather forecast for Saturday, Leo? I want to visit the beach."</i>
                    <br><i>Leo: "Let me check the report. Hmm, it will be sunny in the morning, but it looks like rain in the afternoon over the coast. The temperature will be warm, around 28 degrees."</i>
                    <br><i>Maria: "Oh, so we should go in the morning. What about Sunday?"</i>
                    <br><i>Leo: "Sunday will be windy, so you should bring a jacket. There's a chance of a storm over the lake."</i>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describe weather conditions: 'It will be sunny tomorrow.' Ask about the forecast: 'What's the temperature tomorrow?' Use prepositions of place: 'The rain is in the east.' Engage in peer discussions on weather-related topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What are Maria and Leo talking about?" (Expected answer: Weather and trip plans) "Where does Maria want to go on Saturday?" (Expected answer: The beach)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read sentences about the dialogue and say if they are true or false (e.g., "It will be rainy all day Saturday. - False").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match the day (Saturday/Sunday) with the weather prediction and recommended item (e.g., Saturday - Sunny morning / Rainy afternoon - Go in the morning; Sunday - Windy / Chance of storm - Bring a jacket).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using the Future simple tense for weather predictions ('It will be sunny tomorrow.'), prepositions of place ('The storm is over the lake.'), and modal verbs for recommendations ('It looks like rain today; you should bring an umbrella.'). Vocabulary includes "weather," "forecast," "report," "sunny," "rainy," "windy," "cloudy," "temperature," "will be," "look like," "bring," "on," "at," "in."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion:</b> The teacher provides sentence stems on the board related to weather forecasts. Students complete the sentences orally in pairs using the target grammar and vocabulary. E.g., "Tomorrow, the weather _________ (be) _________ (sunny/rainy)." "If it's _________ (cold), you _________ (bring) a _________ (jacket)." Students read their completed sentences to their partners.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice - Recommendation Chain:</b> Students stand in a circle. The teacher starts by saying, "Tomorrow, it will be sunny. What should we do?" The first student replies with an activity and a recommendation, e.g., "It will be sunny, we should go to the park." The next student builds on this or changes the weather, "Okay, but if it will be windy, you should bring a kite." The chain continues, encouraging the use of different weather vocabulary and modal verbs for recommendations. The teacher ensures intonation is practiced.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction - Weather Map Talk:</b> Students receive a simple weather map showing different weather conditions and temperatures for 3-4 fictional cities (e.g., "City A: Sunny, 30°C", "City B: Rainy, 15°C," "City C: Windy, 20°C"). In pairs, students take turns asking and answering about the weather in each city. Student A asks, "What will the weather be like in City A tomorrow?" Student B replies, "It will be sunny. The temperature will be 30 degrees. If it's sunny, we should visit the beach in City A." They then switch roles and locations.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Weather Report".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-play in pairs or small groups (3 students). Students will plan a hypothetical class trip based on a provided weather forecast for three different locations for "tomorrow."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into pairs or groups of three. Each group receives a "Weather Report for Tomorrow" handout with predicted weather (sunny, rainy, windy, cloudy), temperature, and a recommended activity for three different locations (e.g., Mountain Park, River Beach, City Museum). Example: "Mountain Park: Cloudy, 18°C. Looks like rain over the trees. You should bring a raincoat."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Their task is to discuss and decide on the best location for a trip based on the weather report, making recommendations and clarifying information.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Student A (Trip Planner):</b> Reads the weather forecast and suggests a location. E.g., "I think we should visit the Mountain Park. It will be cloudy, and the temperature will be 18 degrees."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Student B (Activity Selector):</b> Asks questions for clarification and makes suggestions based on the weather. E.g., "Cloudy? Is it sunny or rainy over the mountains? What should we bring? Maybe we should go to the River Beach instead, it will be warm there."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Student C (if group of 3 - Detail Checker):</b> Clarifies details and helps the group make a final decision. E.g., "The report says it looks like rain over the trees at the Mountain Park, so we should bring a raincoat. Or we can go to the River Beach, it will be 25 degrees, but it will be windy on the sand."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The goal is for students to collaboratively discuss their trip plans, justifying their choices with weather predictions and recommendations. They must use the future simple, modal verbs (should), and prepositions of place to describe the weather and locations.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After discussing, each group will briefly present their chosen trip plan and explain why (e.g., "We will visit the River Beach because it will be warm, and we like the sun. We should bring sunscreen and a hat.").</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy (observation checklist, rubric, or peer assessment).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will perform their "Planning a Trip" role-play for the teacher or another pair, presenting their chosen plan and reasoning.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist/Peer Assessment Checklist):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can present weather survey results with visual support (e.g., points to the weather report).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can ask and clarify directions for outdoor activities (e.g., "How is the weather today for going outside?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can discuss trip plans with classmates, based on the weather report (e.g., "Let's visit the park when it is sunny.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses future simple tense for predictions (e.g., "It will be sunny.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses modal verbs for recommendations (e.g., "You should bring...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation is generally intelligible, including intonation in predictions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Engages in peer discussion on weather-related topics.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket before leaving:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you learned to say about the weather today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If it will be rainy tomorrow, what is one thing you should bring?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel discussing trip plans based on a weather report now?" (1-5 scale or thumbs up/down)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of today's lesson helped you speak about the weather the most?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What speaking activities worked best for 6th graders to discuss weather predictions and plans? Did students use the target grammar (future simple, modals for recommendations, prepositions of place) effectively in their discussions? Were there any challenges with pronunciation (intonation, multisyllabic words)? What adjustments or additional scaffolds (e.g., more sentence frames, flashcard prompts) are needed for students who struggled with fluency or accuracy for the next lesson?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on speaking about weather and trip plans. In Lesson 4, students will use this vocabulary and grammar to write a short paragraph about a planned trip, incorporating weather predictions and recommendations.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>